  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Saturday)\n\nUpper pattern continues to be quiet for Saturday and Sunday, but \nchanges are underway as a strong, yet moisture poor upper trough \nprepares to move across the region. Models move the area of \ntighter pressure gradient that was over northern Nevada into east-\ncentral Nevada for the weekend as this trough approaches and \nswings through Monday. This will keep fire weather conditions \nelevated to near critical for winds, as SW winds of 10 MPH to 20 \nMPH with gusts up to 35 MPH will possible for NE Nye, White Pine, \nand SE Elko counties. What will keep Fire weather conditions from \nreaching Red flag criteria is that relative humidity looks to stay\nin the upper teens to upper 20% range through Tuesday, and that \nthanks to recent rains, fuels are not cured. Elsewhere winds will \nbe lighter out of the W-SW at 5 to 15 MPH with gusts up to 25 MPH \npossible. Monday, The GFS and ECMWF have continued to trend a \nlittle bit farther south with the track of the trough, a bit \ndeeper, while continuing to depict a fast moving system. However, \njust as it exits Nevada, this trough will tap into tropical \nmoisture from Hurricane Polo, boosting rain chances farther east. \nThat being said, there will still be a 20% to 50% chance for light\nisolated to scattered showers with a few thunderstorms \naccompanying trough passage Monday. However the best chances at \nseeing rain has trended south across central Nevada. Amounts still\nlook light with a trace to 0.05\u201d for northern Nevada, while \ncentral Nevada may see a trace up to 0.30\u201d near Great Basin \nNational Park. Tuesday and Wednesday shortwave ridging returns \nbringing another period of quiet weather conditions back to the \nSilver State. Thursday into next weekend model agreement still \nlooks rather poor, as models show some form of ridging over the \ngreat basin, but cannot agree on the upper flow as the GFS and EC \nare showing perpendicular solutions as the GFS favors NW flow, \nwhile the EC wants to show SW flow aloft. Temperature wise, a \ncooling trend is underway through Tuesday, with highs falling into\nthe mid 70s. Wednesday will start a warming trend, with the \nreturn of upper ridging to finish out the week as highs return to \nthe mid 80s. Lows will range in the mid to upper 40s for the first\nhalf of the forecast period, cooling into the mid to upper 30s by\nmiddle of next week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \n\nThere is high confidence in a much drier pattern lasting through \nSunday of next week.\n\nThere is moderate to high confidence in the potential for a 20% \nto 50% chance for isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \nacross central Nevada Monday.\n\nThere is moderate confidence in the return of upper level ridging\nfor the middle of next week restoring quiet and dry pattern over \nNevada.",
